WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.700
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.760
It's Stephanie Wong,
Cloud Developer Advocate.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.480
And today I have, again,
Google fellow Eric Brewer.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.160
Here, we have him doing
a series of videos.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.370
We hope that you have
enjoyed them so far.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.910
But Eric, we are both
so excited for this one,

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.970
because we are gathering our
questions from the community.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.720
ERIC BREWER: I am
looking forward to this.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:20.235
It is true.

00:00:20.235 --> 00:00:21.318
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:00:21.318 --> 00:00:23.700
So we have both put out
a prompt for questions.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.800
And this is meant for
anything that you've

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.810
been curious about
around Eric's work

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.180
over the last decade,
any advice that you've

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.369
had for our next
generation of developers,

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.390
especially in the
Kubernetes space.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.410
So first off, the first
question that we've gathered

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.960
is, can we make
use of Kubernetes

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.890
in the edge, namely the
Raspberry API devices,

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.985
where security can
be a big challenge?

00:00:45.985 --> 00:00:48.360
ERIC BREWER: Well, people are
definitely using Kubernetes

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.070
at the edge, mostly in the telco
space and the retail space.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:54.960
Certainly, some
well-known large customers

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.750
actually run Kubernetes clusters
in each of their big stores.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.950
So it's definitely in
use in the edge today.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.880
It also runs on Raspberry
Pi, so that it's not, per se,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.670
new either, although that
is a more constrained way

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.950
to run, for sure.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.460
The implicit question,
though, that it's less secure,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.935
I would say is neither
true nor false.

00:01:16.935 --> 00:01:20.340
I would say Kubernetes
has certainly an option

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.950
to be quite secure, as
secure as other things based

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.080
on, say, traditional VMs.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.320
However, it really comes
down to the whole stack.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.130
So what is the security of
your hardware, for example?

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.340
Do you have any root of
trust in that hardware?

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.160
Google servers that we
put in our data centers

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.212
actually have a hidden
key deep inside so

00:01:41.212 --> 00:01:43.420
that we actually know we're
running on a real server.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.000
We know that the
BIOS is trusted.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.930
We know that the OS is trusted.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.080
So we build up a trusted
stack and run on that.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.330
That's historically
harder to do at the edge,

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.143
but it's still a good goal.

00:01:53.143 --> 00:01:54.060
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.000
Increasingly important as
well, especially given the work

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.850
that you are doing
around bringing security

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.210
to this Kubernetes
and open-source era.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:04.770
Now, what about data?

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.470
So we have another question
about what Kubernetes

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.389
would look like in the--

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:11.250
I'm assuming data
analytics space.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.910
Currently, data applications
are relatively Cloud-specific.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.350
ERIC BREWER:
Certainly, Kubernetes

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.170
started with less focus
on data and more focus

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:22.080
on services, especially
stateless services.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.360
Those are the easiest to
do for lots of reasons.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.050
And that goes, again,
back to the '90s.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.080
That's a basic principle.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.030
But everyone is
building data services

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.370
on communities now
of many flavors,

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.770
and it is certainly possible
now to do a good job of that.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.070
But I think the question
sounds like it's more about,

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:42.600
how do we have the same
kind of revolution for data

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.180
that containers brought to
computing or services brought

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:45.840
to computing?

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.700
And that is a great question,
and it's not easily answered.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.100
But I think the service is
a good framing for that,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.590
meaning that when
I talk about data,

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.520
it's not just the actual data.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.430
It's probably, what
are the methods,

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.170
to use the
object-oriented paradigm,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.110
or the APIs around that data?

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.570
Because if I have an API
view in my data, then

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.023
I also start to
talk about policy,

00:03:12.023 --> 00:03:13.440
who can read it,
who can write it,

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.270
access control kinds of things.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.180
Maybe I can force other
policies like data locality.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.710
This data needs to stay
in Europe, for example.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.740
So I do think we need
a bit better packaging

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.370
of the data itself and
the wrapper around it.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.340
And I would say this
group is exploring that.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.025
But I would say
that's still to be.

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:33.900
STEPHANIE WONG: And
this is related,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.540
but I think there's also
an increasing need for more

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.750
processing power to do things
like machine learning, data

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.010
analytics,
high-performance computing.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:46.440
So what is the future for
co-processors in the Kubernetes

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.208
space, like using
GPUs, TPUs, et cetera?

00:03:50.208 --> 00:03:51.750
ERIC BREWER:
Co-processors in general

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.410
are an important part
of any computing future.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.360
We're at the end of
Moore's law, in the sense

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.400
that general-purpose
computing is not getting

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.660
as fast as it used to per year.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.180
The rate of change is slower.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.310
It's just too bad for all of us.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.710
One of the few ways you
can make up for that

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.200
is to have more
domain-specific hardware.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.630
That's what a TPU
is, for TensorFlow.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:17.130
That's what a GPU is, both for
graphics, and it's used for AI.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.019
So accelerators
are here to stay.

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:22.322
And in fact, you can use both
TPUs and GPUs from Kubernetes.

00:04:22.322 --> 00:04:24.030
There's nothing, per
se, wrong with that.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.600
I would say there's a new
offering of Kubernetes on GKE

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.875
where you can actually subdivide
a GPU into smaller pieces,

00:04:31.875 --> 00:04:34.500
so that it's actually easier to
use a small amount particularly

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.750
for inference, or to use
a large amount if you

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:37.740
want to do training.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.100
But absolutely, that is part of
the core future of Kubernetes.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.060
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.810
And it's been incredible to
see the extension and the usage

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.820
of Kubernetes in the
machine-learning space,

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.110
especially with open-source
platforms like Qflow

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.870
and what we've built
into Vertex AI.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.210
So it's really exciting,
and I'm looking forward

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.610
to seeing where we start
to implement those things.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.110
Now, this question is a common
question that I think we get.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.810
But talking about
on-premise private clouds

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.780
versus public
clouds, to you, what

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.660
is the right balance between
open public cloud and private,

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.730
closed, sovereign
clouds for companies?

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:14.600
ERIC BREWER: That's a
very tricky question,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.030
which many countries
are worried about.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.260
So the short answer
is, you'd be better off

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:22.660
with an open public
cloud pretty much

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:26.230
all the time if you can use
one, because it will have better

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:29.620
cost efficiency, will have
a higher rate of innovation.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:32.290
It can do more things over time.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:36.280
However, that means you must not
only trust your cloud provider,

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.200
but you also have to
trust the government that

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:42.310
runs the country which your
cloud provider is based,

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.810
which for most of us is the US.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.570
So if you're a country that is
worried about the US government

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.240
you may decide, that at least
some of your workloads--

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.700
that's too risky, and
you really want them

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.170
running in your own country.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.545
That's where the
word "sovereign"

00:05:54.545 --> 00:05:56.200
comes from in these contexts.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.825
Now, the good news
is, something that's

00:05:57.825 --> 00:05:59.440
based on open source
like Kubernetes

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.950
is amenable to running
well in private cloud.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:04.450
That's a big part
of Anthos, which

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:05.867
is something I've
worked on, also,

00:06:05.867 --> 00:06:07.720
for both hybrid and multi-cloud.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.210
So you'll see us
talking about sovereign

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.420
in terms of our general
open cloud extension,

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.630
because at least that means
you can use Kubernetes on GKE.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:20.170
And if for some reason you need
to exit, for whatever reason

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.020
you can run Kubernetes on-prem,
and at least your workloads

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:24.130
will be movable.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.608
Now, is it trivial
to do that no.

00:06:25.608 --> 00:06:27.025
But it doesn't
have to be trivial.

00:06:27.025 --> 00:06:28.585
It just has to be possible.

00:06:28.585 --> 00:06:29.710
STEPHANIE WONG: Absolutely.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:30.730
It's a tough space.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:33.370
But it's going to
continue to evolve over

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.410
time as our geopolitical
climate changes.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:39.400
So it's a lot to
think about now.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.250
Now, many, many great
tech, including Kubernetes

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.970
and BigQuery, as you know, came
from internal Google technology

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:47.980
that we've decided
to externalize.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.020
So I think the
community is curious,

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:53.170
but what is the next big
thing that we are hoping to

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:55.597
or we think we
might externalize?

00:06:55.597 --> 00:06:57.430
ERIC BREWER: Well, I
think it just happened,

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:59.140
but people didn't
realize it, which

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:04.420
is Google has great internal
solutions for supply chain

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:05.950
security.

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:07.780
We've written papers on it.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:09.513
VCID is the internal name.

00:07:09.513 --> 00:07:11.680
I don't know what it stands
for, but basically, it's

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.080
a set of invariants
about how our software is

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.760
built, such that
we can prove things

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.500
about it before it operates.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.060
For example, we can
prove internally

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:22.420
that every piece of
code is in our repo

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.670
and that multiple people
have looked at that code

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.108
to make sure that it's safe.

00:07:26.108 --> 00:07:28.150
And those are strong
safeties to be able to make,

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:29.860
and we can make them internally.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:34.720
Now, to externalize that is kind
of like externalizing Bigtable.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.450
We didn't actually release
the actual Bigtable code.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:39.118
If we had, no one
could have used it,

00:07:39.118 --> 00:07:41.410
because it had a lot of
dependencies on internal Google

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:42.280
stuff.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.600
So we wrote a paper.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:45.340
People wrote versions
of it externally,

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.320
leading to HSPACE
and other things.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.040
That's one model.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:53.140
The equivalent here is SLSA,
which is now part of OpenSSF,

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:55.270
where this is a
framework for how

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:56.500
to think about supply chain.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.930
But the good news is, it's
based on what we actually

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:02.135
have been doing internally for,
I think, at least 10 years.

00:08:02.135 --> 00:08:04.010
And we already know
where it's in production.

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:06.910
So like Bigtable, it's
proven in production.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.370
We actually externalize
it with the community.

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:12.710
Because again, we need to
have buy-in on how it works.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:15.203
It needs to be done
in an appropriate way

00:08:15.203 --> 00:08:16.870
for open source, which
is not that easy.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:18.310
Hence, need some discussion.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:20.030
That process is going on now.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:21.970
People absolutely are
welcome to contribute.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.250
It'll also be a big part
of the executive order

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.990
as we move towards
secure supply chains

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.290
in general for the nation.

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:29.290
STEPHANIE WONG: Exactly.

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:31.010
It can't be done in a vacuum.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:32.826
And we are inviting
the community

00:08:32.826 --> 00:08:34.659
to help us bring these
things to the broader

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.530
industry as a whole.

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:38.620
So that is a great
note to end on, Eric.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:41.409
I know that you have shared
your time with us over the span

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:42.667
of this video series.

00:08:42.667 --> 00:08:45.250
So I just want to thank you so
much for sharing your knowledge

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:46.188
and your experience.

00:08:46.188 --> 00:08:47.230
ERIC BREWER: My pleasure.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.840
And great to be
involved in this way.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:50.680
Thank you so much.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.138
STEPHANIE WONG:
For everybody else,

00:08:52.138 --> 00:08:54.070
we hope that you enjoyed
this video series.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.150
Let us know if you
want more like this.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:59.950
And if you do have thoughts,
comments, questions, concerns,

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.230
jokes, check us out on social.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:03.910
Reach out to us on Twitter.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:05.930
We'll include our handles here.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:07.490
Thank you so much, everyone.

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:10.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

